    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. Using the 'note' field for context seems like the safest way to stay compliant with Dublin Core without guessing. \n\nI'm also wondering about the 'subject' or 'description' fields when the content itself is a bit strange or doesn't fit standard geographic or thematic taxonomies. Sometimes these maps depict things that are hard to categorize—like unusual symbols or very localized, non-standard landmarks. How do I handle those 'unknown' or 'unidentifiable' elements in the metadata without it looking like I'm just making things up?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Those kinds of maps can be a real challenge when it comes to metadata. You're right to be cautious about introducing speculative data, but at the same time, you want to provide enough context for researchers to understand the significance of the map.\n\nOne approach is to use the 'subject' or 'description' fields to describe the content of the map in a way that's as neutral and objective as possible. For example, you might describe the unusual symbols as \"geometric shapes\" or \"mysterious markings,\" without trying to assign a specific meaning or interpretation. This way, you're providing enough context to help researchers understand the map, without making any claims that might be disputed.\n\nIt's also worth considering using a 'note' field to provide additional context and explanation for the unusual elements. This way, you can provide a clear record of the information available to you, without introducing speculative data.\n\nAs a side note, I recall a fascinating example of a 19th-century map that depicted a strange, unidentifiable symbol in the center of the map. The map was created by a cartographer who was known for his innovative and experimental approach to map-making. The symbol was never identified, but it's become a kind of legendary feature of the",
      "source": "target",
